  "result": "Neither. \"More agents always give better results\" isn't true, so five versus ten is the wrong question. The right one is whether the assistant needs to be split at all.\n\nTake one request: \"Book the laser cutter Friday, and what's the max material thickness?\" A single agent with three tools (manual search, calendar check, booking) handles it in one loop. Split it into five agents and you add a router, handoffs and extra summaries. Each handoff re-sends context, which costs tokens, and each can lose a detail, like \"Friday\" becoming \"a weekday\". Your budget is small, so that cost matters.\n\n**Why this works:** an agent is worth adding only when it buys something a single agent can't give you. Typical reasons are a task too big for one context window, or independent subtasks that can run in parallel. Your three tools don't obviously meet either test. Booking is also probably better as a fixed workflow than as an agent.\n\nWhy is it tempting? Splitting looks like a tidy org chart, but the model doesn't need one.\n\nTry this: for the makerspace assistant, name one concrete problem a single agent with three tools would hit that a second agent would fix. Can you come up with one?",
